.\" Hand-written on purpose, and it names no flag but --help -- see the note at
.\" the top of issuerd.8 for why, and `make test` for what holds it there.
.TH KERBRIDGE-BROKER 8 "KerBridge" "KerBridge" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
kerbridge-broker \- KerBridge broker daemon
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kerbridge-broker
is the only KerBridge component a workstation talks to. It takes a proof of
cloud identity, decides whether that identity is admitted to this realm, and
asks
.BR issuerd (8)
for a Kerberos ticket-granting ticket on its behalf. The credential cache it
gets back is what the workstation agent injects locally, so the user reaches a
Samba share with no password and no domain join.
.PP
It holds no KDC authority of its own. Everything it can do, it can do because
the issuer agreed to it: the two are separate processes under separate
accounts, and the issuer checks the broker's identity on every connection.
.PP
The proof is validated against the signing keys the cloud identity provider
publishes, fetched over HTTPS and cached. Admission is a group membership in
that provider's directory, not a local decision.
.SH "THE LOOPBACK RULE"
.B kerbridge-broker
serves plain HTTP and is refused any listen address that is not on the loopback
interface. That is enforced in the program, not by configuration, so a
deployment terminates TLS in front of it
.I on the same host \-
terminating on another machine is not possible without a code change.
.PP
Tested Caddy and nginx configurations are in
.IR /usr/share/doc/kerbridge-broker/examples/ .
Both proxy an explicit allowlist of paths rather than everything, so a route
the broker does not serve never reaches it.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B kerbridge-broker
reads the KerBridge configuration set, a directory of TOML files whose default
location is
.IR /etc/kerbridge .
Its own file there is
.IR broker.toml ,
and it reads
.IR realm.toml ,
.I main.toml
and one
.I idp_<source>.toml
per identity provider beside it.
.PP
The set is validated before the daemon starts, by
.B kbconfig check
as
.B ExecStartPre
of the unit below. A set that does not check out keeps this one service from
starting, and affects nothing else; systemd retries five times and then leaves
the unit
.BR failed ,
so the reason stays in
.B systemctl status
instead of scrolling past in the journal.
.PP
With no configuration set at all the unit is skipped rather than started:
.B ConditionPathExists=/etc/kerbridge/main.toml
guards it, so an installed-but-unconfigured host is quiet and
.B systemctl start
still exits 0.
.PP
No credential is ever written in the configuration set. Secrets are files under
.IR /etc/kerbridge.secrets/ ,
named in the set as paths and read through the
.B _kerbridge
group.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/kerbridge/broker.toml
This daemon's own configuration.
.TP
.I /var/log/kerbridge/broker/audit.log
The append-only record of every ticket request and its outcome. Rotated by
.IR /etc/logrotate.d/kerbridge-broker ,
which signals the daemon to reopen the file rather than truncating it under its
feet.
.TP
.I /var/lib/kerbridge/broker/
Durable state: the operator-notification conditions currently outstanding.
.TP
.I /run/kerbridge/issuer.sock
The issuer's socket, opened per request rather than held.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Reopen the audit log. Sent by
.BR logrotate (8)
after it has rotated the file.
.SH OPTIONS
kerbridge-broker takes a small and stable set of options, and this page deliberately
names none of them: a hand-written list is a copy, and a copy is what goes
stale the first time an option changes. Run
.B kerbridge-broker --help
for the set this build has. An argument it does not recognise is refused rather
than ignored, and the refusal prints the same usage line.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR kbconfig (1),
.BR kbmanage (1),
.BR issuerd (8),
.BR kerbridge-sync (8),
